From ed3d0b805a94b58e90ddec083b23ace1529db5f5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 01:35:04 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 26/33] super-amd: rebuild volumes onto spares and returning
 members

When a slot of a managed volume is out of service, mdmon gives md a disk
to recover it onto, as the AMD driver would take one:

- the member's own disk, when it is back (it was missing, or stale): it
  is rebuilt in place, in its extent, as IMSM takes back a returning
  member; a disk failed on errors waits for the user, as with the AMD
  driver, and so does one whose extent another volume holds as well
  (the AMD menu and driver give the extent of an offline member to a
  new volume, clearing the old element; a mix of configurations of
  different generations can still have both);
- else an AMD global hot spare (SpareInfo 0x100), the first free extent
  as large as theirs.  The AMD driver holds a global spare to the
  members' controller only on a platform without arrays across
  controllers, and no platform we have seen is one: any controller will
  do.

The rebuild is recorded the way the AMD driver records it: the transform
log first, then RESTORE_SEGMENTS and the LD it rebuilds into; its
progress goes to the log, and its end puts the spare in the slot.  A
rebuild recorded so - by mdmon or by the AMD driver - goes on after a
reboot from where its log says, exactly where md stopped, when its
target is there as the volume starts.  A target that comes only after
the volume has started is copied from the start, as md may have written
without it (the AMD driver too copies from the start a target md wrote
without).  A former member whose PD record the AMD driver dropped gets
one again.  An mdmon that takes a volume over (--takeover, a restart)
records a rebuild md runs that was not recorded yet, and the end of one
md finished while no mdmon watched.

Written by Claude Opus 5.5 and Claude Fable 5.2, AI models by Anthropic.
